 "prompt": "The repository at /testbed has a bug, described below. Fix it by changing the source code; do not modify or\nadd tests. When you are done, the project's existing test suite should pass.\n\n<issue>\nInconsistent CAST behavior in Spark dialect\n\nI've noticed an inconsistency when using the `CAST` function in the Spark dialect. It seems that the behavior of casting certain data types doesn't align with the expected output, especially when dealing with string conversions.\n\nHere's a simple example to illustrate the issue:\n\n```python\n# Expected behavior\nspark.sql(\"SELECT CAST('123' AS VARCHAR(10))\").show()\n\n# Current behavior\nspark.sql(\"SELECT CAST('123' AS VARCHAR(10))\").show()\n```\n\nIn the expected behavior, the output should be a string with a maximum length of 10 characters. However, the current behavior seems to deviate from this, leading to unexpected results.\n\nThis inconsistency can cause issues when migrating SQL queries from other dialects to Spark, as the expected output might not match the actual output. It would be great to have this behavior reviewed to ensure consistency across different data types and dialects. \n\nHas anyone else encountered this issue, or is there a workaround that I might be missing? Any insights would be appreciated!\n</issue>\n",
